- F-225 **In a self-typed trait with no `comprises` clause, or one that lists only `...`, the compiled checker types `self` in the method bodies as `C[\X\] & X`, the trait met with the implementing type, read from the trait's F-bound; the methods' signatures keep `self` at the plain trait type `C[\X\]`, and the checker refuses an extender at another type.** It builds P-146. A self-typed trait is a trait `C[\X\]` whose parameter `X` extends `C[\X\]`. The checker also takes a parameter bounded by a self-typed trait that the trait extends at that parameter, as the team's `Containment` mixins are: a default of the coordinator's, waiting for the curator's review (review-queue item 212). In a trait whose `comprises` clause lists types, `self` is instead the trait met with the union of those types. For example, `object Bad extends Integral[\ZZ32\]` is refused, since `Bad` is not a `ZZ32`. The rule accepts every self-typed body of the library but `Integral`'s `numerator`, which each integer type declares instead (P-146). When it landed, the distance stage's self-type class fell from 29 errors to 0 (the distance from 85 to 55), one measurement on that tree. Walk has no such rule and loads what it refuses. A compiled default body that returns `self` dies at class load with a `VerifyError` when the trait's self-type parameter is an object type, as in `object Pt(…) extends Shape[\Pt\]`: an open defect (row 695). The rule refuses, rightly, two team declarations, each an open row: the demo `trips.fss`'s `generator` (row 710) and `PureList`'s `List` (row 355). Gated by `compiler_tests/SelfType*` and `XXXSelfType*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-self-type/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2, 4 and 7.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f7/proposals.md, "F-225"); the comprises condition restored, as the text of 2026-10-14 06:09Z had it (SelfParamDisambiguator.scala:77-83, :101-103); the 29 bodies said as the distance sites that the rule cleared at its landing, one measurement (rung-self-type/REPORT.md:3, :75, :132), with P-146's exception, Integral's numerator; P-146's definition of a self-typed trait and its example of a refused extender; after the check: the mixin form restored, as the text of 2026-10-14 06:09Z had it, said as the coordinator's default (SelfParamDisambiguator.scala:101-108; SelfTypeMixinBound.fss; rung-self-type/REPORT.md header; review-queue item 212); the 29 said as the self-type class, the distance falling 85 to 55 (section 4); row 695's condition and open status said; self's type C[\X\] & X and the plain signatures said from P-146; rows 710 and 355 said as open rows
- F-226 **The compiled checker renames an inherited method's own static parameters apart before it uses them: from the static parameters of the trait or object that inherits it, in the overloading check, and from the receiver's static arguments at a call.** So `Generator.generate[\R\]`, inherited by `PossibleReductionPair[\R\]`, keeps its own `R` (row 561). At a call, `commonInheritedMethods` (`scala_src/useful/STypesUtil.scala`) instantiates each method that the receiver's type inherits at the static arguments of its declaring trait. It first renames each of the method's own static parameters whose name those arguments mention to `name$i`, with `i` the least positive integer that gives an unused name; the checker's messages print that form (row 627). The same function builds the methods that a trait's body sees, so a call of an inherited method by its bare name inside a trait gets the same renaming. Gated by `compiler_tests/InheritedMethodStaticParamSameName`, `InheritedMethodByNameStaticParamSameName` and `MethodStaticArgReceiverSameName`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-overloading-checker/REPORT.md` section 6 (the overloading check); `compile-ladder/rung-checker-expected-type/REPORT.md` section 1 (the call).
  by: the clarity pass; note: the detail repointed to the reports that hold each fix: rung-overloading-checker/REPORT.md section 6 for row 561 (climb batch 8 rung O) and rung-checker-expected-type/REPORT.md section 1 for row 627 (climb batch 11 rung E), in place of perf-probes/nat/triage.md section 3, which classifies the capture, and rung-checker-contexts/REPORT.md section 1, which holds row 651 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f7/proposals.md, "F-226"); after the check: the overloading check's renaming said apart from the inheriting trait's parameters (OverloadingChecker.scala:214-216; row 561); the arguments said as the declaring trait's static arguments from the receiver's type, name$i's i said, commonInheritedMethods located, and the premise of the bare-name call given (STypesUtil.scala:1850-1876; rung-checker-expected-type/REPORT.md section 1)
- F-227 **The compiled checker replaces a closed trait among an intersection's conjuncts by those of its listed types that no other conjunct excludes, only where that excluding conjunct names no type variable or inference variable, so `K ∩ R[\T\]` stays as written; below a union, an inference variable takes the whole union as one upper bound, and a type variable is tried through its bound as well as against each member.** The code is `normConjunct` and, for the union cases, `pSubInner`, in `scala_src/types/TypeAnalyzer.scala`. So a call through a parameter bounded by a closed trait and a bound that excludes one of its listed types checks, and a generic declaration so bounded, beside a declaration on the closed trait, is a valid overloading (rows 686, 687, 689, 690 and 711). A call whose argument's type only carries the parameter, `Box[\T\]`, under a ground second bound, stays refused: an open defect (row 712). Before the checker kept such an intersection as written, it accepted, through the expansion, an overloading that it now refuses, on a question that Appendix I lists as not settled (row 713, contested; `compiler_tests/XXXOverloadTwoBoundsClosedTraitExclusion` pins the refusal). Gated by `compiler_tests/*TwoBoundsClosedTrait` and `*TwoGroundBounds*ClosedTrait`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 6.
